{{RunningHeader|zygosphene||}}
of the vertebræ of serpents and some lizards, which fits into a corresponding fossa, the zygantrum, on the posterior part of the neural arch of a preceding vertebra, and serves thus to interlock the series of arches more effectually than would be done by zygapophyses alone. Compare cut under ''zygantrum.''
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Anterior face of a dorsal vertebra of the python, showing ''zs'', zygosphene; ''pz'', prezygapophysis; ''tp'', transverse process; ''ns'', neural spine; ''nc'', neural canal; ''c'', centrum of the procœlian vertebra, whose concavity fits the convexity of the centrum shown under ''zygantrum''.}}}}

'''zygosporangium''' (zī′gọ̄-spọ̄-ran′-ji-um), ''n.''; pl. ''zygosporangia'' (-ä̤). [NL., < Gr. ζυγόν, yoke, + σπορά, seed, + ἀγγεῖον, vessel.] In ''bot.'', a sporangium in which zygospores are produced.

'''zygospore''' (zī′gọ̄-spōr), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ζυγόν, yoke, + σπορά, seed.] In ''bot.'', a spore formed in the process of reproduction in some algæ and fungi by the union or conjugation of two similar gametes or protoplasmic masses: called ''isospore'' by Rostafinski. Also ''zygosperm'', ''zygote''. See ''spore''<sup>2</sup>, ''conjugation'', 4 (with cut).
